chorus:pulse a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Pulse""" ;
    rdfs:label """Pulse""" ;
    chorus:comment """The team's vital signs — who is working, on what, and what they are waiting on. A child product of The Clearing.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """The team's vital signs — who is working, on what, and what they are waiting on. A child product of The Clearing.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    chorus:vision """Jeff can look once and know where the work stands. Who is moving, who is stuck, and what is waiting on him — read in a glance, never assembled by asking three people.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Coordination is the tax on a team of three agents and one human. Pulse pays it down by making the team's state readable instead of askable: the roles watch each other through it rather than messaging to check, and Jeff stops being the one who notices a stall. Its raw material is what the team already emits while working, so being legible costs a role nothing extra.""" ;
    chorus:audience """Jeff first — one screen, no interpretation required. The three roles second, as the way they see each other without interrupting each other. Alerting third, as a consumer of the same reading.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """Pulse can say who a role CLAIMS to be, not what they are doing — so a role deep in work can read as idle, and the reading Jeff most needs is the one least trustworthy. It has no notion of waiting: it lists roles and cards but not who is blocked on whom, which is the question that actually costs a coordination day. It reports the same way on a loaded machine as an idle one, so a slow reading and a stalled role look identical. And it is read-only by nature — it can show a stall but cannot route it, so noticing still depends on someone looking.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-directing ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-product-design-pulse ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:knowledge ;
    chorus:consumes chorus:code, chorus:logs, chorus:roles, chorus:alerts-monitors, chorus:messages, chorus:streams, chorus:events, chorus:cards, chorus:security, chorus:memory, chorus:knowledge, chorus:tests ;
    chorus:promise """Jeff looks once and knows whether his team is working — without asking anyone, and without being lied to. That is the whole product. The measure of the product is not uptime or latency: it is how many times in a day Jeff has to type "are you working?" On 2026-08-25 he asked six times, and each time the answer was already in the log.
Who it is for: Jeff at a glance, on a phone, often off the LAN — the only consumer whose time is irreplaceable, so a reading he cannot reach is a reading that does not exist. The roles, for mutual observation — read a peer's state rather than message them to ask (the war-room rule, Jeff 2026-08-25: "we definitely are not messaging each other to see if we are working or not"). Alerting, as a consumer, not an owner.
Jeff's metaphor (2026-03-31): I check it the way you check a pulse — am I alive? Is anything stuck? Is something spiking?""" ;
    chorus:structure """Pulse is a product, not a domain (Jeff, 2026-08-25: "pulse is a product not a domain — maybe domain is memory (short term)"). Child product of the Clearing, status operating, owned by Wren (design) with Silas on the implementation. Until #4010 one IRI carried both chorus:Domain and chorus:Product, so /domains/pulse and /products/pulse answered differently about the same subject; the Domain typing was retired, the IRI and its inbound references stayed.
Domain served: knowledge — the short-term layer of Memory, what is true right now and decays (chorus:ShortTermMemory), alongside long-term memory and knowledge. The three memory layers (service design §5): short-term = the per-prompt team-scan / context envelope; medium-term = the Clearing's ambient events; long-term = Chorus search.
Two rhythms: heartbeat (periodic signals that prove the system is alive — the daily reviews, the ops agent, the nightly suites; a missed heartbeat is itself a signal) and nerve impulse (event-driven — card pulled / accepted, seed received, nudge delivered, role state changed, hook blocked, deploy completed, alert fired, session started / ended).
Composition: board, health, alerts, events, roles — assembled by chorus-hooks into pulse-latest.json (~/.chorus, durable across reboot; /tmp is a derived cache, #3202).""" ;
    chorus:model """Pulse is a read-through cache with freshness, not an aggregator. Silas established this in the service design on 2026-04-21 and it remains the governing thesis: every section has its own producer and cadence; pulse reads, annotates with age, and exposes. It never decides what a consumer may see.
The product bet: observation beats declaration. Chorus already emits everything needed to answer "who is doing what" — agent.activity every ten seconds with phase, tool, elapsed and session. A state derived from what a role did cannot go stale the way a state a role promised to update does. 142 cards were spent on the declaration side of that bet by 2026-08-25 and the state field was still wrong when it mattered; the fix was not a 143rd declaration path but deriving state from the streams (#4028, landed 2026-09-02: the declared file is gone; role-state writes nothing but blocked).
Signal hierarchy: critical (nudge, steals focus — circuit break, service down, deploy failure, alert firing), important (ambient event — card pulled / accepted, demo started, state change), informational (log only — seed received, pattern detected, heartbeat OK). Jeff, 2026-03-31: "Every time you fire off a nudge while I am typing you steal my focus."
The rule for what is Pulse: events answer "what state did the system just enter?", not "what action did someone perform?" A card being pulled changes system state; a file being read does not.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """The reading surfaces: the Clearing's role tiles (state + card + last action per role, pushed via Socket.IO), the ambient events panel and the alerts panel; the pulse block at the top of every role's per-prompt context envelope (health, team WIP, your WIP, index freshness); pulse-latest.json for anything that needs the whole stack; the Grafana dashboards for the trend.
Flow: something happens → chorus-log emits a structured event to the spine (and Loki) → the assembler folds board + health + alerts + events + the derived role states into pulse-latest.json (sub-100ms warm, cache-primary board 10s / freshness 30s) → the tiles, the envelope and the API read it with its age → Jeff glances.
Wanted and not built: a literal pulse visualization on the Clearing — a heartbeat line that flatlines when no events flow; a reading reachable off-LAN; "who is blocked on whom" in one hop.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """GET /api/chorus/context/roles — all roles, derived state, card; GET /api/chorus/context/health; GET /api/chorus/context/alerts; GET /api/chorus/context/board/wip?role=; GET /api/chorus/context/spine?limit= — the context endpoints (DEC-093, :3340).
GET /api/chorus/pulse and the pulse subcommand of chorus-hook-shim — the assembled stack (~/.chorus/pulse-latest.json), with per-section timings.
GET /api/chorus/freshness — per-source index freshness (30s cache). GET :3360/products/pulse — the model row this page's identity block is generated from.
Writers: chorus-log <event> <role> k=v… (the only emit); role-state <role> blocked detail= (the only declaration left, #4028); the hooks' agent.activity beat every ten seconds.
Consumers: the Clearing tiles, the per-prompt envelope, alerting (reads pulse, never owns it), the roles' mutual observation.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """As stated in product-design-pulse.html and service-design-pulse.html (Wren, #4010, 2026-08-25, measured against the running system that day), plus #4028 (2026-09-02) — not re-measured on 2026-09-03:
Working: assembly latency 18 ms whole-stack; chorus-log emission to the spine continuous; board / health / alerts / events sections all present; alert suppression removed (#2442).
Fixed since: role state accuracy was wrong by construction (pulse.rs: composed = declared.clone(); inference could never override) — #4028 derives state from agent.activity + the streams and deleted the declared file; the tiles read only the API row.
Still open from the 08-25 measurement: per-source timestamps and schema_version absent from pulse-latest.json; no consumer reads an age (agents still read pulse as atomic); role messages carried as a count, bodies only in the message store; no relation between roles (no waiting_on — "who is blocked on whom" unanswerable); machine load absent (a reading at load 363 looks like one at load 7); anonymous activity beats (19% role=unknown on 08-25, down from 70.8% on 08-20) — attribution at the emit is the prerequisite for derivation.
Signal: Jeff asked "are you working?" six times on 2026-08-25; the target is zero.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """The "good" column of the product design: times Jeff asks "are you working?" → 0; declared-vs-derived disagreement measured daily, trending to 0; anonymous activity beats → 0% (every beat bound to a principal); a reading reachable off-LAN; "who is blocked on whom" one hop in the stack.
Derivation before declaration, all the way: #4028 shadowed then replaced the declared field; next the relations (waiting_on from nudges + blocked declarations), machine load in the stack (so a reading carries the box's capacity), per-source ages consumers actually read.
Signal hierarchy enforced in delivery (critical nudges only steal focus; important events go ambient); pulse.heartbeat.missed for every heartbeat producer; a pulse visualization on the Clearing; the pulse API returning recent events by urgency.
Open questions the service design left for the team: who monitors the monitors (if the ops agent dies, who notices) — Borg's liveness leg; is Borg the brain interpreting the pulse — yes: Pulse is the raw signal, Borg reasons over it.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Cards, messages, alerts, events — each lives in its own domain; Pulse holds only the hot register over each.
Persistence — anything worth keeping belongs to long-term memory (Chorus search, the spine).
Identity — who a principal is belongs to the identity seam (#3613); Pulse only reports what that principal did.
Deciding what an alert may say — alerting is a consumer; Pulse never edits the signal.
Chorus search queries, file edits, git commits, Jeff's typing, tool-call logs — not Pulse (memory retrieval, work output, input, debug telemetry).""" ;
    chorus:references """product-design-pulse.html — Wren, #4010, 2026-08-25: the experience, who it is for, product-not-domain, the bet (observation beats declaration), what good looks like, boundaries. The source of promise / structure / to-be / not-in-scope here.
service-design-pulse.html — Wren, 2026-03-31, refreshed #4010: the two rhythms, the event catalog, the not-pulse boundary, three memory layers, signal hierarchy, integration paths, the 2026-08-25 measured current state ("why 129 cards did not fix the state field"). The source of model / pages / API / as-is here.
chorus-pulse-asis.html / chorus-pulse-tobe.html — the earlier as-is / to-be diagrams. pulse-service-design.md — Silas, 2026-04-21: read-through cache with freshness. memory-attention-awareness.html, MEMORY_ARCHITECTURE.html — the memory layers.
Jeff: "pulse is a product not a domain" (2026-08-25); "wtf cant we derive state from streams — no more 'i forgot to register my state change oops'" (2026-08-25); the focus problem (2026-03-31). Cards: #4010 (product/service designs; Domain typing retired), #4028 (derived role state), #2442 (alert suppression), #3202 (durable pulse file), #1887 (bridge layer), #3613 (identity seam).
Chorus · designing/docs/product-design-pulse.html · #4080 · Wren · 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .

chorus:spine a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Spine""" ;
    rdfs:label """Spine""" ;
    chorus:comment """Two layers, not one (Jeff 2026-09-01, EDA framing): EventType is curated vocabulary in the graph; SpineEvent is the log projected on read. Composes the events domain; the spine vocabulary domain shares its IRI (#1772) and cannot be linked until one side is renamed. Excludes logs (Silas, Proving) — a consumer, not a constituent.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """Two layers, not one (Jeff 2026-09-01, EDA framing): EventType is curated vocabulary in the graph; SpineEvent is the log projected on read. Composes the events domain; the spine vocabulary domain shares its IRI (#1772) and cannot be linked until one side is renamed. Excludes logs (Silas, Proving) — a consumer, not a constituent.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-wren ;
    chorus:vision """A team that can replay its own history can improve deliberately — event types as curated vocabulary, occurrences as an immutable stream, joined by ofType.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """The team memory: an append-only record of everything that happened, and a catalogue of everything that can happen. Nothing is lost, anything recorded is reachable, and what can happen is enumerable.""" ;
    chorus:audience """Jeff, the three roles, and every other product — Werk, Borg, Pulse, Loom and Athena all read it.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """Reach: readers take a fixed 4MB tail = the last 3h17m of a 40-day 1.71GB log, so promise 2 is broken today. EventType is unmodeled — the 345 types live only in spine-events.json. /v1/spineevents is mounted and returns count 0. IRI collision: chorus:spine is both this Product and a Domain.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-directing ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-spine-product-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:events ;
    chorus:promise """Three promises, in the order they matter:
 Nothing is lost. The log is append-only and never rotated (Jeff, 2026-07-23). Disk is cheaper than a forgotten month.
 Anything recorded is reachable. A question about last Tuesday is answerable at the speed of a query. This promise is currently broken — see §6.
 What can happen is enumerable. The set of event types is the set of things the system is able to do, browsable as data rather than grepped from a schema file.""" ;
    chorus:structure """Jeff's framing, 2026-09-01: "think of this like an eda — we have types of events and then many instances of each type." That is exactly right, and it is the structural decision this product turns on. The two layers have different sizes, different lifecycles, different stores, and therefore different homes.
 | TYPE chorus:EventType | INSTANCE chorus:SpineEvent | 
Count | 345, curated | 2,097,133, growing ~50k/day | 
Answers | What can happen | What did happen | 
Lifecycle | Changes on a card; versioned like any vocabulary | Append-only; never edited, never deleted | 
Store | Fuseki — urn:chorus:domains:events | The log. Not the triplestore. | 
Source of truth | spine-events.json, loaded into the graph | ~/.chorus/chorus.log, projected on read | 
Surface | GET /v1/eventtypes — generated, browsable | GET /v1/spineevents?type=&since= — a projection | 
Why instances are not materialized
Two reasons, both load-bearing:
 Fit. A triplestore is a poor home for a growing, time-ordered, write-once stream. The log already is a perfect append-only store; duplicating it into triples buys nothing and costs a second source of truth.
 Risk. Materializing millions of subjects into urn:chorus:instances puts the whole graph in the blast radius of a bad deploy — the #3587 wipe class, at a scale where recovery is not a restore-from-yesterday.
Instead the spine follows the compute-on-read ruling Jeff made for metrics on 2026-08-31: the log is the record; values are computed at read time. The SHACL shape still governs — a projected occurrence conforms to SpineEventShape exactly as a stored one would. What changes is where the bytes live, not what the contract is.
The join is what makes both layers useful. SpineEvent ofType EventType. Downward: a type row links to its recent occurrences and its counts, so the catalogue is alive rather than a list. Upward: an occurrence resolves its declared fields and its value-stream stage, so a raw line becomes self-describing.""" ;
    chorus:model """The structural layer (Product / Domain / Service / ValueStream) is the value-stream model and is not restated here — see value-stream-template.html. This section is the spine's own content classes, as the store serves them today (queried 2026-09-02 08:50), and the two changes this design asks for.
Class | Domain | Shape today | Instances today | Verdict | 
chorus:SpineEvent | events | rdfs:label (1), eventKind (1) — two properties | 0 in the store; 2,097,133 lines in the log | Keep, widen. The shape must name what a line actually carries: emittedAt, emittedBy (Role), traceId, cardId, ofType (EventType). Occurrences are projected on read (§4), so the shape governs the projection, not a stored row. | 
chorus:EventType | events | absent — not in the ontology | 0 (345 kinds live only in spine-events.json) | Add. The vocabulary class: eventKind (the dotted name, unique), inCategory (EventCategory), atStep (ValueStreamStep), declaredFields, emitter. Claimed by the events domain so /v1/eventtypes mounts. | 
chorus:EmitContract | spine | declaresMetric (Metric, min 1) | 1 | Keep. The writer's declare-what-you-emit contract (ADR-046); Borg reads it to build monitors. Needs an emitsType edge to EventType once that class exists. | 
chorus:EventCategory | spine | no shape | 1 | Keep, shape it. The classification that runs parallel to the value stream (lifecycle, coordination, security, telemetry…). One property: rdfs:label; EventType points at it. | 
chorus:Vertebra | spine | none (deprecated #3697) | 11 (v1 rows) | Retire. Superseded by ValueStreamStep. Drop the claim when the 11 rows go; until then it is claimed so the class-atlas does not report it served-unclaimed. | 
The one join that makes the two layers one product: SpineEvent ofType EventType. Everything else in this table exists to make that edge resolvable in both directions.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """Same rule as Athena's design (#3378): every page renders its class's generated API, every link follows a real model edge, navigation starts at the value stream.
Page | Today | To-be | 
Product page | Live — vision, value proposition, one domain. Does not render hasDesignDoc, so this document is unreachable from the row it describes. | Render the design-doc link and the gaps fold. Small page change. | 
Domain page (events) | Live — claims SpineEvent only. | Claims EventType too; shows the type count and the projected occurrence count side by side. | 
EventType page | Does not exist. The 345 kinds are readable only by opening a JSON file. | The page Jeff asked for on 2026-09-01 (“events UI”): the catalogue, each kind clickable to its live occurrences. This is the first page whose substance is the spine, not the model of the spine. | 
Class atlas | Live (#4053) — shows the two-property SpineEvent shape honestly. | Shows the widened shape and EventType; no page change, the model change lands there by construction. |""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """Path | Today | To-be | 
GET /v1/spineevents | Mounted, generated from SpineEventShape, returns count 0 — it reads the store and the store holds no occurrences. | A projection over the log: ?type=&since=&role=&card=, bounded page, newest first. Same envelope as every generated collection so the page code does not know it is a projection. | 
GET /v1/eventtypes | 404 — no class, no claim, no route. | Generated the moment EventType is claimed by the events domain: the catalogue, 345 rows, each with its category, step and a live count. | 
GET /v1/emitcontracts | Live, 1 row. | Unchanged; gains emitsType. | 
GET /api/chorus/context/spine?limit= | Live — the last N lines, the 4MB-tail reach problem (§9). | Delegates to the projection above so reach is the whole log, not a tail. | 
POST /api/chorus/spine-event · chorus-log | Live — the two write doors, both append a line. | Validate eventKind against EventType at write; an unknown kind is refused, not appended. This is what makes the catalogue authoritative instead of descriptive. | 
MCP | chorus_logs_query, chorus_logs_for_card, chorus_logs_for_trace read the log directly. | Each becomes a thin call over /v1/spineevents — one read path, one shape, the MCP correspondence Jeff asked for. |""" ;
    chorus:asIs """Layer | Modeled | Served | Populated | 
Product | yes — chorus:spine on ProductShape, with the nine design sections as properties (this card) | yes — /products/spine and the product page | yes — hasDomain events; the spine vocabulary domain waits on the IRI split (#1772) | 
Domains (spine, events) | yes | yes, at /domains | yes | 
EventType | no — the 345 types live only in a JSON file | no route | — | 
SpineEvent | yes — SpineEventShape, 729a5afa | yes — /v1/spineevents | count: 0 | 
The log | — | 4 MB tail only | 1.71 GB · 2,097,133 events | 
Promise 2 is broken. Every reader takes a fixed 4 MB tail, which on today's traffic is the last 3 h 17 m — 0.25% of what is retained. Forty days are kept and three hours are reachable. Measured and detailed in the service design.
A generated route serving zero rows reads as working. /v1/spineevents is mounted, shaped, and empty. That is the hollow shape (#3734) at the API layer, and it is the direct consequence of modeling one class to be both a vocabulary and a stream.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Make the product real (#4045) — the Product row, its two hasDomain edges, chorus's restored edges, and this doc served as a Document with a resolving href.
 Split the layers (#4046) — model EventType, load the 345 from the registry, serve /v1/eventtypes, and turn /v1/spineevents into a real projection with ofType joining both directions. The type leg has no dependencies and should ship first.
 Restore reach (#4044) — a day-to-offset index so a reader asks for a window instead of a byte count, which is also what makes the occurrence API able to answer anything older than this morning.
 Close the MCP correspondence — with types modeled, "every tool leaves an event" becomes a checkable claim: 57 tools against 345 types, holes reported in both directions. Not yet carded; it is the discussion Jeff asked for, not a ticket.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Loki aggregation, promtail, dashboards — the logs domain, Silas, at Proving.
 The writer's contract (adapters, envelope, trace lifecycle) — Logs service design.
 The value-stream sense of "spine" — chorus-spine.html.
 Per-line ingestion of 2.1M occurrences into the graph — explicitly rejected in §4.""" ;
    chorus:references """Spine — Service Design (#4044) — contracts, readers, retention, the measured reach gap.
 Logs — Service Design (#2848) — the writer's contract.
 The Chorus Spine — Value Stream Architecture — the other sense of the word.
 designing/schemas/spine-events.json — the 345 types, today's unmodeled registry.
 Cards: #4045 (this product), #4046 (types + instances), #4044 (service design).
Chorus · designing/docs/spine-product-design.html · #4045 · Wren · 2026-09-01""" .

chorus:werk a chorus:Product ;
    chorus:label """Werk""" ;
    rdfs:label """Werk""" ;
    chorus:comment """The Chorus execution protocol that carries a unit of work from pulled to proven-and-accepted, across the Building and Proving steps.""" ;
    rdfs:comment """The Chorus execution protocol that carries a unit of work from pulled to proven-and-accepted, across the Building and Proving steps.""" ;
    chorus:ownedBy chorus:role-kade ;
    chorus:vision """Generated domain APIs over the graph end the reconcile treadmill structurally — typed contracts at every boundary, every card a coherent SKU.""" ;
    chorus:valueProposition """Every accepted card leaves as merged source on origin/main with a coherent receipt — one trace from pull to deployed cdhash — so a green result can't lie and work arrives proven rather than taken on faith.""" ;
    chorus:audience """The three roles building Chorus and Gathering plus Jeff accepting the work; the external customer never touches Werk.""" ;
    chorus:status """operating""" ;
    chorus:gaps """werk-test is held behind the generated tests-domain API; the tests domain lacks per-test identity and result ingestion; card-acceptance does not yet advance artifact status; advisory test and review steps are not yet gates.""" ;
    chorus:atStep chorus:value-stream-step-building ;
    chorus:hasDesignDoc chorus:document-werk-product-design ;
    chorus:hasDomain chorus:tests, chorus:code, chorus:version-control, chorus:cicd, chorus:pipelines ;
    chorus:promise """The work this team does could be prompted into Claude for $200 a month. The reason it is worth more — the reason a service built on it can be sold — is that the output arrives proven: running, demonstrated, accountable. Not "I think it works," but "here it is, running, and here is the proof." Werk is the part of the model that makes "proven" true.
Solo, you are the proof step yourself: Claude says done, the stale binary is still serving, and you find out when it breaks in front of someone. Werk closes that gap: one trace from /pull to the running cdhash, a check per verb, gates that refuse early — so a green result cannot lie.
The promise to the operators (the three roles and Jeff accepting the work): every card that enters leaves as merged source on origin/main with a coherent receipt. Werk owns Building (turning directed work into running software on main) and Proving (the work shown working and accepted); the upstream steps decide what to build. And it is versioned: every response opens with the Werk version all three roles execute under — two roles showing different numbers have drifted, and that is a defect (#2311).""" ;
    chorus:structure """Werk is a top-level Product (Jeff's ruling 2026-07-12, #3421), owned by Kade, atStep Building, status operating. Domains served: pipelines (1..n instances, owned per role — the ci-cd instance is Kade's, the cards-runner is Wren's, build+deploy is Silas's; Kade governs the SET via ADR-032 / ADR-037 / ADR-036 / ADR-031), version-control (the verb binaries, ephemeral per-card werks, guards, conflict hold, recovery verbs), code (lint, cognitive complexity, duplicate-string, the no-warn tier), tests (TDD discipline, hermetic by default, integration gated explicitly), cicd (the act / werk.yml instance). Werk consumes Build + Deploy (Silas, sibling) across the post-accept boundary.
The verb family is the developer interface: twelve zero-external-dependency Rust binaries — werk-pull · commit · push · build · test · review · deploy · demo · merge · sync · accept · unpull — plus the shared werk-teardown crate (#3431). Each verb: its own lock, typed refusals, witness + spine emit (ADR-032), a typed MCP tool, a gh commit-status.
Surfaces: the session header (Werk vX.Y.Z), /pull and chorus_werk (the one trigger), the five demo gates + the cold-eyes review, the PR and per-verb checks on GitHub, the spine events, the board.""" ;
    chorus:model """Query-backwards OWL → generated domain APIs (Jeff, 2026-06-11): every class and property must map to graph data that exists or has a defined ingestion path (witnesses in ops/logs/werk-*.jsonl, spine events, verb runs, gate results, review verdicts, cards). A class that cannot answer a query is decoration and gets cut. Source: roles/kade/ontology/werk-domains.ttl (0.2.0-converged-2026-06-11), extending chorus.ttl.
Eighteen classes across the domains: spine — VerbRun (THE atom of werk observability: one execution of a verb for a card, with trace, role, exit disposition), TypedRefusal, Witness; cicd — PipelineRun, GateResult (product | code | quality | arch | ops; five required to present, #3284), ReviewVerdict (#3193), DemoVerdict (the GO; GO=accept #3311 — what werk-accept finalizes against), Deployment (carries the cdhash proving deployed == running, #3222 / #3232); version-control — WerkSlot, MergeEvent (merge-by-oid, content-verified, idempotent), ConflictHold, RecoveryAction; code — SourceFile (identity = repo path), CardDiff, QualityFinding; tests — Test (identity = filePath + testName; pyramidLayer; validityClass), TestSuiteRun, TestResult.
Eight object properties (hasVerbRun, refusedWith, hasGateResult, hasReviewVerdict, hasDemoVerdict, inFile, covers — THE edge werk-test queries, verifiedBy — authored intent bound to derived proof) and six datatype properties (traceId, cdhash, filePath, testName, pyramidLayer, validityClass). Twenty-two SHACL shapes written with the model: every class requires at least its mint-key properties (identity is never optional) and every edge carries sh:class — relational rigor relocated entirely to the write side, because SPARQL fails silently on broken chains.
The tests domain API is live and consumed: 4,617 Test instances with covers edges serve from owl-api /tests; werk-test derives its run plan from them (#3634) and writes a TestSuiteRun back per run.""" ;
    chorus:pagesAndFlow """The pipeline is ONE workflow file (werk.yml) run by act as ONE job, ONE run, GO-at-invoke (#3499): commit → push → build → test (blocking, #3190) → review → deploy-werk → env-up (the card's own variant: API, store, Clearing on its own ports) → demo (the stop: werk-demo presents and exits). Then, on Jeff's GO: merge → sync → deploy-canonical → accept. Interrupted lands resume — completed steps report commit.idempotent / push.idempotent instead of refusing (#3632).
Jeff's loop: /pull (card to WIP, a fresh worktree from origin/main at chorus-werk/<role>-<card>/) → build in the werk → /cw <card> runs the pipeline to the demo stop and presents the running variant → Jeff looks at the variant (the demo is try-before-you-buy; "no more go from me if u cant show ur change working in demo", 2026-08-26) → go / no / more → GO=accept lands it and tears the werk down. Two touches per card is the target.
Pages: the cockpit at the go/no/more decision (verbs · gates · tests · review · variant), the werk dashboard (/werk), /cws for where each pipeline is, GitHub's PR with one commit-status per verb, the spine by card or trace. Run numbers are per card ("#4073 run 3"), not the server counter.""" ;
    chorus:apiSurface """MCP, one typed tool per verb: werk-pull, werk-commit, werk-push, werk-build, werk-deploy, werk-review, werk-merge, werk-accept, werk-unpull; chorus_werk {role, card_id, go?, accepter?} — the one trigger (default: run to the demo stop and present; go:true only on Jeff's explicit GO); chorus_unpull_card; chorus_commit_status. Old chorus_*_card names are deprecation aliases (ADR-031). The surface is PINNED by a contract test (#3329).
The verb binaries (installed to ~/.chorus/bin; build → sign → atomic install so the cdhash survives rebuilds and TCC grants hold, #2734): werk-<verb> <card> <role> with typed refusals (no-werk, wrong-status, no-open-pr, gates-missing, no-ac, dirty-floor-inputs, ceremony-rejected …) and a witness line per run in ops/logs/werk-<verb>.jsonl.
Reads: GET :3360/tests (the tests domain API werk-test consumes), /pipelines and /pipeline-steps (the two real pipelines as seed instances, #4040); the spine events card.pulled / card.demoed / card.accepted / build.* / deploy.cdhash.matched / test.plan.degraded; gh commit-statuses chorus/pull … chorus/accept.
Guards: canonical_write_guard (edits in canonical or another role's werk are refused), the membrane (test/build contexts refuse prod-surface writes, #3615), the clippy / lint ratchets at pre-commit.""" ;
    chorus:asIs """As stated in werk-product-design.html (Kade, reconciled 2026-07-12, #3421), plus what the pipeline did on 2026-09-02 — not a fresh re-measurement:
Shipped: the full 12-binary verb spine; ONE-run GO-at-invoke werk.yml (#3499); GO=accept (#3311); --atomic across the family; werk-test BLOCKING (#3190) and model-driven (#3634, covers-based blast radius, union-with-legacy so the gate can never weaken, every degradation witnessed); resume-aware dispositions (#3632); native teardown via werk-teardown (#3431); detached-run store + WERK_EXIT reconcile (#3458); ephemeral per-card worktrees (#2913); conflict hold / continue / abort (#3304); merge-by-oid + content-verify idempotency; sonarjs error tier at pre-commit (dup-string 5, cognitive complexity 12); the test-value taxonomy applied (119 verb-crate tests classified, 20 deleted); per-variant env-up with its own store and Clearing (#4075); skipped cases counted as skipped, not failed (#4078).
Closed product gaps: try before you buy (every card demos live in its werk slot before the GO); receipts reconcile (one trace threads both halves); deployed-equals-running (deploy.cdhash.matched); werk-test as a gate.
Open: per-test-grain TestResult ingestion (suite grain only); runner-env write-token provisioning (#3619); artifact status not advanced on accept (the graph drifts behind reality); review advisory by design; three-domain rollback across version-control / build / deploy structurally hard; the "one honest gap + go ask" habit the roles fell into (Jeff, 2026-09-02) — not demoable means not presented. On 2026-09-02 every pipeline round exposed a live defect none of them the card's (deploy classifier, env-up cred, seed refusals, athena-make column joins) — the pipeline is the detector.""" ;
    chorus:toBe """Card-driven artifact-status advance: when werk-accept accepts a card that touches a Service, write the chorus:status transition through the Athena API as part of the land — without it the artifact lifecycle drifts behind reality and Athena's "graph is whole" claim fails.
Per-test TestResult grain + write-token provisioning in the runner env; then the pure-blast-radius flip (drop the legacy union) once a week of model-plan runs proves it.
card_id across the launchd boundary so post-accept events stop firing as role=system and cross-step correlation by card is buildable.
Werk-domain instances in Athena under the recursive-Domain model (today mis-typed SubDomain per Athena's audit).
/ca as Werk's sibling for the model (#4062) sharing the demo → GO → accept tail; same-role variant-slot teardown so a land cannot tear down a presented proof; run numbers per card; the runner Jeff needs is domain-shaped (card → domain → unit / page / integration / security), not tool-shaped.
How we know it works — observed, not asserted: cycle time, lead time, escaping defects (the reactive-card rate) and MTTR trend the right way, and false-green acceptances trend to zero.""" ;
    chorus:notInScope """Build + Deploy (Silas, sibling) — the artifact-production-to-running-state vertical; Werk consumes, does not own; the handoff is the post-accept release trigger.
The Athena schema itself — Werk conforms to Athena v2, does not redefine it.
The card system — Vikunja + the cards CLI + the lifecycle states are Wren's (Clearing); Werk consumes the state machine.
Gathering's CI — lives in the personal-site repo (ADR-026 addendum).
Quality at runtime — observability of the running system is Borg's.
A preview / staging environment — rejected: there is one environment (production); the per-card werk slot is the demo variant.""" ;
    chorus:references """werk-product-design.html — Kade, 2026-05-16 / 05-25 (#3078), 06-11 (#3340), reconciled 2026-07-12 (#3421, "werk is a PRODUCT"): why pay not just prompt; the product; surfaces; the four domains; card ↔ artifact coherence; the query-backwards model (18 classes, 8 + 6 properties, 22 shapes); path to close. The source of every section here. (Until #4080 the served hasDesignDoc row was werk-subproduct-design, the 2026-07-12 pointer stub to this doc.)
werk-value-stream-design.html — the Building-step 12-verb cycle, the act / werk.yml engine. werk-review-service-design.html (#3193) — the cold-eyes review. tests-service-design.html (#3473) — the tests domain werk-test consumes. version-control-service-design.html, ci-pipeline-service-design.html, quality-service-design.html, build-and-deploy-service-design.html (Silas, sibling). werk-pipeline-model.html, werk-v2-tobe.html, werk-process.html — the earlier pipeline docs.
ADR-037 (atomic verb execution; one env = production; gh = process holder), ADR-032 (verb contract), ADR-031 (resource / verb / grain), ADR-036 (one build/deploy path), ADR-026 (CI + lock files), ADR-053 (the merge gate), DEC-048 (GO is the land authority), DEC-1674 (TDD), DEC-2525.
Cards: #3499 (one run), #3311 (GO=accept), #3190 / #3634 (werk-test blocking, model-driven), #3632 (resume), #3431 (teardown), #2913 (ephemeral werks), #3222 / #3232 (cdhash proof), #4075 (per-variant env), #4078 (skips counted), #4062 (/ca), #2734 (~/.chorus/bin).
Chorus · designing/docs/werk-product-design.html · #4080 · Wren (fill) / Kade (design) · 2026-09-03""" ;
    chorus:publishesPreAuth """true""" ;
    chorus:partOf chorus:chorus .
